   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the social services law and the state administrative procedure act, in relation to additions to the list of medicaid reimbursable nonprescription drugs",
    "basePrintNo": "S7517",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7517-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §365-a Soc Serv L; amd §102, St Ad Proc Act",
    "lawSection": "Social Services Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7517\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the social services law and the state administrative\nprocedure act, in relation to additions to the list of medicaid\nreimbursable nonprescription drugs\n\n\n PURPOSE :\nTo expand the availability of safe, cost-effective medications under\nthe Medicaid program by providing a \"fast track\" for adding\nnonprescription drugs to the list of Medicaid reimbursable\nnonprescription drugs in Health Department regulations.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n§ 1 amends Social Services Law §365-a(4)(a) to provide that additions\nto the list of drugs reimbursable under the Medicaid program may be\nfiled as regulations without prior notice and comment.\n\n§2 adds a new subparagraph (xiv) to § 102(2)(a) of the State\nAdministrative Procedure Act (SAPA) providing that \"any regulation\ncomprised solely of one or more additions to the list of\nnonprescription drugs reimbursable under the medicaid program\" is not\nincluded within the definition of a \"rule\".\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nThe New York State Medicaid program currently provides coverage for\nall prescription drugs.  The Social Services Law also allows Medicaid\nreimbursement for nonprescription drugs that are specifically listed\nin the Health Department's regulations. This policy recognizes that\nsome medications available over the counter are as safe and effective\nas similar prescription drugs, but may be beyond the reach of\nlow-income patients. If a nonprescription product is not on the\nreimbursable list, a health care provider might opt to treat a patient\nof limited means with a more expensive prescription drug which might\nnot be more effective but is Medicaid reimbursable.\n\nThe Health Department must go through the SAPA rulemaking process to\nmake changes to the list. This includes preparation of supporting\ndocumentation, publishing a notice of proposed rulemaking in the State\nRegister and receiving comments for at least 45 days. The final rule\ncannot be effective until a notice of adoption is published in the\nRegister. Also, since the list is currently included within SAPA's\ndefinition of a \"rule\", executive review under Executive Order\n20 can further delay additions to the list. In limited cases the\nDepartment may be able to justify acting through emergency rulemaking,\nbut still must complete all of the above steps to finalize the rule.\n\nThere is little need for prior public review and comment when a new\nitem is added to the list of reimbursable nonprescription drugs. The\nDepartment has already determined that the product is beneficial and\ncost-effective, and no one is required to prescribe the\nnonprescription drug. Providers use their own professional judgment on\nwhich prescription and nonprescription drugs are best for their\npatients.\n\nThis bill merely provides a \"fast track\" to speed up the process for\nmaking new nonprescription products an option under Medicaid. Other\nchanges to delete or restrict currently-listed drugs would remain\nsubject to ordinary notice and comment rulemaking under SAPA.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\nA.8859 (2004) - referred to assembly health committee\nA.4191/S.4892 - (2005-06) - passed Assembly, referred to Senate Social\nServices, Children & Families Committee\n\nA.2265/S.3883 (2007-08) - passed Assembly, referred to Senate Social\nServices, Children & Families Committee\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nSavings from allowing health care providers to treat patients covered\nby Medicaid with safe, cost-effective nonprescription medications on\nan expedited basis.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect on the 30th day after it shall have become\nlaw.\n",
